Angle 1: The science of consciousness is fracturing — and that strengthens the case
The consciousness science landscape in 2025–2026 is defined by a single watershed event: the COGITATE
adversarial collaboration, published April 30, 2025 in Nature. Funded by $20 million 
 from the
Templeton World Charity Foundation, this experiment pitted Integrated Information Theory (IIT) against Global
Neuronal Workspace Theory (GNWT) using 256 human participants across seven laboratories. The results were
inconclusive for both theories. IIT's predictions about posterior cortex activity partially held (2 of 3
preregistered predictions passed), but its predicted sustained synchronization was not confirmed. GNWT fared
worse — 0 of 3 predictions passed threshold. As Anil Seth observed: "No single experiment would decisively
refute either theory."
This matters for the CSGA thesis because the lack of scientific consensus on consciousness makes
governance more urgent, not less. If scientists cannot agree on what consciousness is, then creating systems
that may instantiate it without any governance framework is reckless. Four additional adversarial collaborations
are now underway, including INTREPID 
 (IIT vs. Predictive Processing) and ETHOS (Higher-Order
Theories), but results will take years. The governance gap cannot wait.
The Eon Systems fruit fly emulation sits at the exact fault line. The foundational peer-reviewed paper — Shiu et
al., "A Drosophila computational brain model reveals sensorimotor processing," Nature 634: 210–219, 2024 —
demonstrated that a leaky integrate-and-fire model of 
 139,255 neurons and 50 million synaptic
connections 
 could predict motor behavior with up to 95% accuracy. 
The March 2026 embodied demonstration (integrating this brain with the NeuroMechFly v2 body in MuJoCo
physics) 
 produced foraging, grooming, feeding, and escape behaviors 
 — but it has
not yet been peer-reviewed as a standalone publication. Tyler Cowen noted on Marginal Revolution: "N.b. this
work is not yet published." 
 Eon's own team cautions their results "should
not yet be interpreted as a proof that structure alone is sufficient to recover the entire behavioral repertoire."
