Angle 5: Defense policy is blind to brain emulation — but legislation is beginning to notice
No international law addresses brain emulation technology in defense contexts. The Geneva Conventions define
combatants as "persons," raising unresolved questions about whether emulated minds would qualify for
protections. The Martens Clause — requiring weapons to comply with "principles of humanity and dictates of
public conscience" — provides the closest existing legal hook for consciousness-based prohibitions, but it has
never been tested against digital minds.
However, a legislative signal emerged: S.2604 (119th Congress), 
 incorporated into the FY26
NDAA, mandates establishment of an AGI Steering Committee co-chaired by the Deputy Secretary of Defense
and Vice Chairman of the Joint Chiefs. This committee must assess neuromorphic computing and cognitive
science applications 
 — the first legislative acknowledgment that brain-like AI requires specific
defense policy attention. The report is due 
 to Congress by January 31, 2027. 
DARPA's programs reveal the military's deep investment in brain-derived AI. The MICrONS program ($100
million) reverse-engineered one cubic millimeter of the brain 
 and produced the largest co-
registered neurophysiological/neuroanatomical dataset from mammalian brains. 
 The NESD
program aims for implantable neural interfaces communicating with 1 million neurons. 
 The L2M
program develops biologically-inspired lifelong learning machines. 
 The neuromorphic computing
defense market was valued at $1.9 billion in 2024, projected to reach $8.4 billion by 2033. 
The ICRC warned in August 2025 that bidirectional brain-computer interfaces "may risk recasting combatants
as components of weapon systems." 
 The SCL's five prohibitions — no weaponization of consciousness,
no forced labor of sentient AI, no deceptive deployment, no consciousness suppression, no exploitation of
emergent properties — find no direct equivalent in existing defense law, but each is defensible under the
Martens Clause and the principles of distinction and proportionality in international humanitarian law.
 The fact that no defense framework addresses these questions while brain emulation
advances from flies to mice creates precisely the governance vacuum the CSGA framework is designed to fill.
